<!-- BEGIN orca-kanban -->
## Orca Kanban board

When the user says "add to the kanban", "add a card", "kanban this", "put this on the
board", "queue this", "break this into cards", or asks what is on the board, use the
`kanban` CLI. Run `kanban help` for the full command list.

- Add work: `kanban card add "<title>" --description "<detail>" --acceptance "<checkable outcome>"`
- New cards default to **Backlog** (staging). Only use `--state Ready` when the user
  asks to run it.
- Several pieces of work → one card each, ordered with `--deps <id,id>`. Never one fat card.
- Read the board: `kanban card list`, `kanban card show <id>`, `kanban status`.
- Run it: `kanban run --once` (one card) or `kanban serve` (board UI + auto-run).
- Fix stuck work: `kanban card retry <id>`, `kanban card move <id> <state>`, `kanban recover`.
- `kanban card resume <id>` reopens a finished card's agent conversation (stored per
  worktree), so a closed terminal is not a dead end during review.
- A schedule needs `--state Ready`: a date on a Backlog card never fires, because
  Backlog is not picked up. `kanban card list` says why a Ready card waits.
- Later or repeating work: `--not-before 7d` on add, `kanban card snooze <id> 1w`, or
  `--every 1w` for a recurring check. A held card is not eligible until it is due.

A finished card waits in **Review** for a human. `kanban card approve` and
`kanban card reject` are the human's verdict — never run them on your own work.
`kanban card diff <id>` and `kanban card open <id>` help a human review; when they
dictate a rejection, pass their reason through verbatim with `-m`.

"Create a ticket" / "FR" / "Jira" means **Jira**, not this board.

**If the current directory or any parent contains `.orca-kanban/card.json`, you are
the agent executing that card.** Work only on that card and never modify the board;
put follow-up work in your summary instead. The CLI refuses board writes there
(exit code 3) — do not pass `--force` to bypass it.
<!-- END orca-kanban -->
